; status: works — swivel rabbit ear, free hinges (not the incenter case,
; contrast examples/bases/rabbit-ear.bel). The two hinge creases land at an
; arbitrary height on the side edges rather than at the triangle's bisector
; angles, so the crease that flattens the vertex is not constructible by any
; Huzita axiom from the named points — `flatten`'s derive mode solves for it.

paper square

mark --v = map .a onto .b     ; x = 1/2: spine, also the fold's symmetry axis
.m = --v * --cd               ; apex (1/2, 1)

step precrease
mark --_am = through .a .m    ; scaffolding — incenter only locates the hinge height
mark --_bm = through .b .m
mark --_ba = map --ab onto --_am
mark --_bb = map --ab onto --_bm
.o = --_ba * --_bb

.lowerp = --_ba * --_bm
mark --lowerh = perp --bc through .lowerp   ; a height off the incenter, not a bisector

mark --ba = through .a .[--bc --lowerh]     ; hinge from a, free height (not through .o)
mark --bb = through .b .[--da --lowerh]     ; hinge from b, same free height

step ear
; only THREE of the four rays at the hinge vertex are given (the away rays of
; --ba, --bb, and the downward ray of the spine --v); flat-foldability forces
; the fourth — the emergent "ear" crease, a mountain at ~320° from the vertex
; (meeting the base right of center), not parallel to any given line (verified:
; docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-15-flatten-generalizes-collapse-design.md).
--ear = flatten (--ba \ .a) (--bb \ .b) (--v \ .m) {toward .c}
